---
import CapabilityGrid from '../../components/CapabilityGrid.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ProjectDetailCTA from '../../components/ProjectDetailCTA.astro';
import ProjectDetailHero from '../../components/ProjectDetailHero.astro';
import { aiGatewayCapabilities, aiGatewayDifferentiators } from '../../data/ai-gateway';
---

<Layout
  title="AI Gateway — Conscia Labs"
  description="AI Gateway manages model access, routing, limits, and costs for applications and teams. It also offers hosted MCP tools such as WebSearch. Closed beta."
  noIndex
>
  <div class="project-page">
    <ProjectDetailHero
      name="AI Gateway"
      category="Artificial Intelligence"
      type="Platform"
      description="Give people and applications access to approved AI models. Set limits and keep track of who used what, and what it cost."
      lede="Model access your team can manage."
      status="AI Gateway is in closed beta. Talk to your Conscia representative if you’d like to try it."
      externalHref="https://console.ai.conscialabs.com"
    />

    <section class="detail-overview section-light" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
      <div class="container detail-overview__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">01 / How it works</p>
          <h2 id="overview-title">Keep model access in one place.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-overview__copy">
          <p>A request comes into Gateway. It checks the caller’s access and limits, picks an allowed model and provider route, and records usage and cost. Audit records show how the request was handled.</p>
          <p>Clients can use OpenAI-style or Anthropic-style APIs. The features you get depend on the model and its qualified route. Not every provider supports the same things.</p>
          <p>AWS Bedrock is the supported pilot integration. The OpenAI, Anthropic Direct, custom OpenAI-compatible, GitHub Copilot, and Conscia Sovereign AI Cloud adapters are in Preview. You can only use models that are enabled and have qualified routes.</p>
          <p>Gateway isn’t an assistant app. Beacon and other products handle the conversations, context, and interface around it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <CapabilityGrid
      items={aiGatewayCapabilities}
      heading="What Gateway handles."
      introduction="The model API doesn’t run tools for you. Hosted MCP servers give clients a separate way to use tools such as WebSearch."
    />

    <section class="detail-overview section-light" aria-labelledby="security-engine-title">
      <div class="container detail-overview__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">03 / Security Suite — Preview</p>
          <h2 id="security-engine-title">Security Enforcement Engine.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-overview__copy">
          <p>The Security Suite’s separate engine is in Preview, outside the standard release. When enabled, it observes a copy of chat input the gateway has already accepted. The model request doesn’t wait for the inspection result.</p>
          <p>It’s observation-only. Inspection results don’t block requests or change what gets delivered. Output inspection, enforcement modes, and advanced detection aren’t available in this preview.</p>
          <p>The gateway’s mandatory safeguards and your organization’s access policies still apply. They’re separate from this optional observation service.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="differentiators section-light" aria-labelledby="differentiators-title">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro section-intro--light">
          <div>
            <p class="section-label">04 / What sets it apart</p>
            <h2 id="differentiators-title">Admins and developers have different jobs.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Admins manage the rules and limits, while developers use the model API. Products such as Beacon provide the interface and workflows around it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="differentiator-grid">
          {aiGatewayDifferentiators.map((item) => (
            <article class="differentiator" style={`--item-accent: ${item.accent}`}>
              <span class="differentiator__number">{item.number}</span>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.description}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="portal-preview section-dark" aria-labelledby="portal-title">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="portal-preview__intro">
          <div>
            <p class="section-label">05 / Developer Portal</p>
            <h2 id="portal-title">Find a model and try it.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Manage your keys, test requests in the Sandbox, and follow a quickstart. You can check usage and limits there as well.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="portal-preview__figure">
          <img src="/images/ai-gateway/developer-portal.png" alt="AI Gateway Developer Portal showing setup progress, available models, usage, and quickstart actions" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Developer Portal overview</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <ProjectDetailCTA
      label="06 / Closed beta"
      description="AI Gateway is in closed beta. What you can use depends on the enabled services and qualified model routes. Talk to your Conscia representative if you’d like to try it."
      externalHref="https://console.ai.conscialabs.com"
    />
    <Footer />
  </div>
</Layout>
